15.2 Project Overview

Use this as the content:

Project Title

221B — Holmes Personal Intelligence System

Concept

221B is a personal intelligence and investigation management interface designed around the working style of Sherlock Holmes.

The system combines daily scheduling, investigation management, evidence organisation, tasks, locations and notifications into a single interface.

The central UX concept is that Holmes should not need to manually coordinate every part of his working day. The system should identify important information and bring potential conflicts or urgent investigations to his attention.

Main Objective

The objective is to design an interface that allows the user to:

view the daily schedule
manage investigations
monitor urgent cases
organise evidence
record deductions
manage tasks
monitor important locations
receive notifications
detect scheduling conflicts
15.3 Target User
Sherlock Holmes

Occupation: Consulting Detective
Location: 221B Baker Street, London
Interface type: Personal intelligence and investigation management system

Sherlock Holmes was selected because his activities involve multiple simultaneous investigations, rapid analysis, meetings, evidence collection and unpredictable changes to his schedule.

The interface therefore focuses on information prioritisation and intelligent scheduling rather than simply presenting a conventional calendar.

15.4 User Goals

The interface was designed around these goals:

Goal	UX Requirement
Know what needs attention	Prioritised dashboard
Manage daily activities	Schedule
Handle urgent investigations	Urgent case notifications
Avoid schedule conflicts	Conflict detection
Analyse evidence	Deduction Board
Track investigations	Case management
Manage actions	Task management
Find investigation locations	Location management
Review important messages	Notifications
15.5 User Limitations

These are contextual limitations, not medical assumptions.

High workload

Holmes may manage several investigations simultaneously.

Unpredictable cases

An urgent investigation may appear while another activity is already scheduled.

Information overload

Investigations can contain large amounts of evidence, observations and people of interest.

Rapid decision-making

Important decisions may need to be made quickly.

Frequent travel

Holmes may move between 221B Baker Street, Scotland Yard and crime scenes.

Competing priorities

Routine activities may need to be interrupted when a critical investigation appears.

Complex reasoning

Evidence must be connected to observations, suspects and deductions.

15.6 Main UX Problem

The main problem identified is:

How can Sherlock Holmes manage a changing daily schedule while simultaneously tracking multiple investigations and making rapid decisions from large amounts of information?

The interface addresses this through:

Prioritisation + intelligent notifications + conflict detection + structured investigation information.

