15.8 Primary User Journey

Your main UX journey is:

Urgent Investigation Received
             ↓
      Notification
             ↓
         Dashboard
             ↓
        Review Case
             ↓
       Case Details
             ↓
      Review Schedule
             ↓
    Schedule Conflict
             ↓
    Conflict Assistant
             ↓
     Review Alternatives
             ↓
   Reschedule Existing Event
             ↓
     Confirm New Schedule
             ↓
       Updated Schedule

This is the main story of your UX assignment.

15.9 Intelligent UX Feature
Schedule Conflict Detection

This is your strongest unique UX feature.

Suppose Holmes already has:

14:00 – 15:30
Evidence Analysis — Case #27

A new urgent investigation arrives:

14:30 – 16:00
Scotland Yard Investigation

The system calculates:

Existing event
14:00 ───────── 15:30

New event
14:30 ───────────── 16:00
       ↑
       60-minute overlap

Instead of simply allowing the conflict, the interface informs Holmes:

Schedule conflict detected

and provides:

Reschedule existing event
Accept both events
Cancel new investigation

This demonstrates proactive UX rather than passive information display.

15.10 Usability Testing

For the assignment, test the prototype using another person.

Test participant

Use an actual participant and record:

Participant ID
Age range
Occupation/student status
Previous experience with web applications
Date of test

Avoid unnecessary personal information.

Testing scenario

Give the participant this scenario:

You are using Sherlock Holmes' personal intelligence system. An urgent investigation request has arrived from Inspector Lestrade. Review the case, check Holmes' schedule, identify the scheduling conflict and resolve it without losing the urgent investigation.

Tasks

Ask the participant to:

Find the urgent investigation.
Open Case #31.
Review the case information.
Open the schedule.
Add the new investigation at 14:30.
Identify the schedule conflict.
Review the available options.
Reschedule the existing event.
Confirm the updated schedule.
Open the Deduction Board.
15.11 Feedback

Don't invent feedback.

Create a table and fill it after you actually test:

Task	Participant Action	Difficulty	Feedback	Change Required
Find urgent case				
Open Case #31				
Review schedule				
Detect conflict				
Resolve conflict				
Open Deduction Board				

This is important because your lecturer can see that the design was iterated based on testing, rather than simply claiming that users liked it.

