15.13 Visual Design
Colour system
Background       Near black / charcoal
Primary surface  Dark zinc
Text             Ivory / light grey
Accent           Muted brass / amber
Critical         Amber
Urgent           Burgundy / red
Secondary        Muted grey
Typography

Use:

Serif typography for major headings
Sans-serif typography for interface controls and data

This creates a visual connection to the Victorian Sherlock Holmes setting while maintaining modern usability.

Design principle

The interface deliberately avoids excessive Victorian decoration.

The goal is:

Victorian character + modern intelligence interface

rather than making the application look like a historical museum website.

15.14 Responsive Design

The interface supports:

Desktop
Sidebar + Header + Main Content
Tablet
Collapsible navigation
Flexible content grids
Mobile
Hamburger
     ↓
Mobile navigation
     ↓
Single-column content

The design should remain usable at approximately:

390 × 844
15.15 Accessibility Considerations

Document these:

sufficient text/background contrast
clear button labels
icons accompanied by meaningful labels where appropriate
consistent navigation
visible focus/hover states
responsive layouts
readable typography
important information communicated through text, not colour alone
15.16 Conclusion

Your conclusion should explain that the interface was designed around the specific workflow and contextual demands of the selected user, rather than simply creating a generic dashboard.

The most important UX contribution is the combination of:

Daily Schedule
      +
Investigation Management
      +
Information Prioritisation
      +
Conflict Detection
      +
Deduction Organisation